WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.830
Put four counters into three boxes.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.190
You can place the first three with one in each box.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.550
The fourth has to join a counter somewhere.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.530
If you already put two together earlier,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.130
the claim already holds.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.130
This is the pigeonhole principle.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.500
A guarantee asks what must happen in

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.730
every possible case.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.880
Imagine the arrangement that delays the result for as

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:23.570
long as possible.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.080
Its limit helps us find the smallest count that will

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:27.890
force the result.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.670
The important decision is what the boxes represent.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.090
A drawer contains many socks of exactly four colours.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.150
You take socks without looking.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.840
What is the smallest number you must take to guarantee

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.590
two of the same colour?

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.910
Try to plan a draw that delays the first matching pair.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.830
Use one box for each colour.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.920
Begin with one sock in each box.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.520
That is a possible draw of four socks with no match.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.180
The fifth sock has to go into one of the occupied boxes.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:04.090
Five is sufficient, and this example with four proves

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:05.460
that five is the minimum.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.520
The boxes can stand for remainders.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.440
Take any four different whole numbers.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.080
When we divide by three, the possibilities are zero,

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:15.640
one and two.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.140
Two of the four numbers share a remainder.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:19.460
Take their difference.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.040
The leftover parts cancel, leaving a multiple of three.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.700
Choose six different numbers from one to ten.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.350
Group the ten numbers into five pairs that

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:30.140
add to eleven.

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:33.430
A choice of six puts two numbers in one of those

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:34.520
five pair boxes.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.030
Both members of that pair have been chosen,

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.860
and they add to eleven.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.860
Choose the objects and choose the boxes.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:43.220
Compare their counts.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.940
When the problem asks for the smallest guarantee,

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.160
show how one fewer could fail.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.650
In your notebook, how many people must be in a room to

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.860
guarantee two birthdays in the same month?
